# UPSSSC JA 3284 exam-pattern passages (English): easy matter, short words, almost no numbers.
EN = [
("Netaji Subhas Chandra Bose", "Subhas Chandra Bose is one of the most loved heroes of our freedom struggle. He was born in Cuttack in Odisha and was a bright student from his early days. He passed the tough civil service exam in England, but he gave up the post because he did not want to serve the British rule. He joined the Indian National Congress and soon became a strong voice of the youth. He was twice elected as the president of the Congress. He believed that freedom would not come by requests alone and that India must fight for it. He left the country in secret and went to Germany and then to Japan to seek help. In South East Asia he took charge of the Indian National Army, which was made up of Indian soldiers and common people. His famous call, give me blood and I will give you freedom, filled the hearts of people with courage. He also gave the slogan Jai Hind and Chalo Delhi. He gave women a place in the army through the Rani of Jhansi regiment. People called him Netaji with love. His life teaches us courage, unity and love for the nation."),
("The Indian National Army", "The Indian National Army was formed to free India from British rule with the help of arms. It was first started by Mohan Singh and Rash Behari Bose in South East Asia. Later Subhas Chandra Bose took its command and gave it new life. Many Indian soldiers who had been held as prisoners of war joined it, and so did a large number of Indians living in Malaya, Burma and other lands. Netaji set up a free Indian government and gave the army a clear aim, to march to Delhi. The army fought bravely in hard conditions near the eastern border of India. It faced lack of food, heavy rain and disease, yet its soldiers did not lose hope. The army could not win the war, but its story stirred the whole nation. When some of its officers were put on trial at the Red Fort, people from every corner of the country stood with them. The trials made it clear that the British could no longer rely on Indian soldiers. In this way the Indian National Army gave a strong push to the final stage of the freedom movement."),
("Deepawali, the Festival of Lights", "Deepawali is the festival of lights and one of the biggest festivals of India. It is celebrated with great joy in the month of Kartik. It is believed that on this day Lord Rama came back to Ayodhya after fourteen years of exile, and the people lit rows of lamps to welcome him. Weeks before the festival, people clean and paint their homes and shops. Markets are full of sweets, clothes, toys, lamps and gifts. On the day of Deepawali, people wear new clothes and worship Goddess Lakshmi and Lord Ganesha in the evening. Every house shines with clay lamps, candles and colourful lights. Families share sweets with friends and neighbours, and children enjoy the bright lights. The festival teaches us that light always wins over darkness and good always wins over evil. However, we should celebrate it in a safe and clean way. Loud crackers make the air dirty and harm old people, small children and animals. We should use clay lamps, buy goods made by local workers and share our joy with the poor. Then Deepawali will truly bring light to every home."),
("Holi, the Festival of Colours", "Holi is the festival of colours and joy. It comes in the month of Phalgun, when winter ends and spring arrives. The festival is linked with the story of Prahlad, a young devotee of Lord Vishnu. His father, the cruel king Hiranyakashipu, tried many times to kill him. His sister Holika sat in a fire with Prahlad in her lap, but the fire burnt Holika and Prahlad came out safe. To remember this victory of good over evil, people light a bonfire on the night before Holi. On the next day, people play with colours and coloured water. Friends, neighbours and even strangers smear each other with gulal and say Happy Holi. People forget old fights and meet each other with open hearts. Special sweets like gujiya are made in every home. The Holi of Mathura, Vrindavan and Barsana is famous all over the world. We should play Holi with dry and natural colours, because some colours have harmful chemicals. We should not waste water and should not force colour on anyone who does not want it. Holi teaches us love, unity and the joy of a new season."),
("Veer Kunwar Singh", "Veer Kunwar Singh was a brave leader of the revolt of eighteen fifty seven. He was the ruler of Jagdishpur in the Bhojpur region of Bihar. When the revolt began, he was already an old man of nearly eighty years, yet he took up arms against the British with great courage. He led his soldiers in many battles in Bihar and Uttar Pradesh. He was a clever leader who used surprise attacks and quick moves to defeat a much larger army. It is said that while he was crossing the Ganga, a British bullet hit his arm. To stop the poison from spreading, he cut off his own arm with his sword and offered it to the river. Even after this, he fought on and won back his fort at Jagdishpur. He died soon after his return, but his name became a symbol of courage. Folk songs of Bhojpur still sing of his bravery. His life shows that age is no bar for those who love their land. Every year people remember him with respect and pride."),
("Review of a Mobile Phone", "I bought a new mobile phone last month and I have used it every day since then. The phone looks smart and feels light in the hand. The screen is large and bright, and videos look clear even in sunlight. The battery is the best part of this phone. With normal use it easily lasts for a full day and a half, and it charges fast. The phone runs smoothly when I use social media, make video calls and watch news. Games also run well, but the phone gets a little warm after long use. The main camera takes good photos in daylight with nice colours. In low light, the photos are fine but not great. The front camera is good for video calls. The sound from the speaker is loud and clear. The phone has a fingerprint lock and face unlock, and both work quickly. One weak point is that it comes with some extra apps which I had to remove. Overall, this is a good phone for students and office users who want a long battery life and a clear screen at a fair price."),
("Review of a Book", "I recently read a book of short stories about village life, and I found it very touching. The writer tells the stories of farmers, women, children and old people in a simple and honest way. The language is easy, so even a young reader can enjoy it. Each story is short, and you can finish one in a few minutes. The best story is about an old man who waits every day for a letter from his son in the city. It shows the pain of families who are left behind when young people move away for work. Another story about a girl who wants to go to school made me smile and think at the same time. The writer does not give long lectures. Instead, he lets the people in the stories speak for themselves. Some stories end in a sad way, but they always leave a ray of hope. The book also paints a lovely picture of fields, rivers and festivals. I would have liked a few more stories about the changes in villages today. Still, this is a warm and thoughtful book that I would suggest to all readers."),
("Review of a Small Restaurant", "Last Sunday I went with my family to a small restaurant that has opened near the bus stand in our town. The place is clean and simple, with bright lights and wooden tables. The staff greeted us with a smile and gave us water and the menu at once. The menu is not very long, but it has a good mix of north Indian food, snacks and sweets. We ordered paneer curry, dal, rice, hot rotis and a plate of mixed vegetables. The food came within a short time and was served hot. The paneer was soft and the curry had a nice taste without too much oil. The dal was simple and tasty, just like home food. The rotis were fresh and soft. The only thing we did not like was the sweet dish, which was too sugary for us. The prices are fair, and a family of four can eat well without spending too much. The restaurant could add a few more seats, as it gets crowded in the evening. On the whole, it is a good place for a simple and clean meal with the family."),
("Raksha Bandhan", "Raksha Bandhan is a festival that celebrates the bond of love between brothers and sisters. It falls on the full moon day of the month of Shravan. On this day, sisters get up early, wear new clothes and prepare a plate with a rakhi, roli, rice, a lamp and sweets. They tie the rakhi on the wrist of their brothers and pray for their long and happy life. In return, brothers give gifts to their sisters and promise to protect them always. The festival is not only for brothers and sisters by birth. Many people tie rakhi to friends, soldiers and even trees to show love and care. There are many old stories linked with this festival. One famous story tells how Queen Karnavati sent a rakhi to Emperor Humayun to ask for help. Markets are full of colourful rakhis and sweets many days before the festival. Sisters who live far away send rakhis by post, and brothers wait for them with joy. Raksha Bandhan reminds us that love, trust and care are the real strength of a family and of the whole society."),
("Chhath Puja", "Chhath Puja is a holy festival celebrated mainly in Bihar, Jharkhand and eastern Uttar Pradesh. It is also celebrated with great faith in cities across India wherever people from these regions live. The festival is devoted to the Sun God and Chhathi Maiya. It lasts for four days and comes a few days after Deepawali. The people who keep the fast are called vratis, and most of them are women. They keep a very strict fast and do not even drink water for a long time. On the third evening, the vratis stand in a river or pond and offer prayers to the setting sun. On the last morning, they offer prayers to the rising sun and then break their fast. The ghats are cleaned and decorated with lights, and families carry baskets of fruits, sugarcane and thekua, a special sweet. Folk songs of Chhath fill the air with a sweet and holy feeling. The festival teaches us discipline, purity and respect for nature. It also brings the whole community together, as people help each other in cleaning the ghats and making all the arrangements."),
("Dussehra", "Dussehra, also called Vijayadashami, is celebrated on the tenth day after the nine nights of Navratri. It marks the victory of good over evil. According to the Ramayana, Lord Rama killed the demon king Ravana on this day and freed Sita. In many parts of the country, the story of Rama is shown on stage for many days through Ramlila. On the day of Dussehra, huge figures of Ravana, his brother Kumbhakarna and his son Meghnad are made and burnt in open grounds. People gather in large numbers to watch this sight. In other parts of India, the festival marks the victory of Goddess Durga over the demon Mahishasura. In Bengal, people bid farewell to the goddess by taking her idols to rivers. The Dussehra of Mysuru and Kullu is famous for its grand processions. On this day, people also worship their tools, books and vehicles and begin new work. The festival teaches us that pride and evil must fall in the end. It inspires us to remove the bad habits within ourselves and to walk on the path of truth."),
("Republic Day", "Republic Day is one of the most important national festivals of India. It is celebrated every year on the twenty sixth of January. On this day, the Constitution of India came into force, and our country became a republic. The day was chosen to honour the call for complete freedom made by the Congress on the same date many years before. The main event takes place on Kartavya Path in New Delhi. The President hoists the national flag, and the national anthem is played. A grand parade shows the strength of our army, navy and air force. Colourful tableaux from different states show the rich culture of India. School children perform dances and songs. Brave soldiers and children who have shown great courage are honoured. Jet planes fly over the sky and leave trails in the colours of the flag. The day is also celebrated in schools, offices and villages across the country with flag hoisting and cultural programmes. Republic Day reminds us of the rights given to us by the Constitution and also of our duties towards the nation."),
("Independence Day", "Independence Day is celebrated every year on the fifteenth of August. On this day, India became free from British rule after a long and hard struggle. Thousands of brave men and women gave their lives so that we could live in a free country. On this day, the Prime Minister hoists the national flag at the Red Fort in Delhi and speaks to the nation. The speech talks about the work done by the government and the plans for the future. In schools, children take part in parades, sing patriotic songs and give speeches about our freedom fighters. Sweets are given to everyone. Buildings are decorated with lights in the colours of the national flag. Kites can be seen flying in the sky in many cities. People remember great leaders like Mahatma Gandhi, Bhagat Singh, Subhas Chandra Bose and Sardar Patel. Independence Day is not just a holiday. It is a day to think about what freedom means. We must respect the law, help others, keep our country clean and work hard to make India strong and happy."),
("Rani Lakshmibai of Jhansi", "Rani Lakshmibai was the brave queen of Jhansi and one of the greatest heroes of the revolt of eighteen fifty seven. Her childhood name was Manu, and she learnt horse riding, sword fighting and shooting from an early age. She was married to the king of Jhansi. After the death of her husband, the British refused to accept her adopted son as the heir and tried to take over Jhansi. The queen said that she would never give up her Jhansi. When the British army attacked the fort, she fought bravely along with her soldiers. Many women also fought by her side. When the fort could no longer be saved, she escaped on horseback with her son tied to her back. She then joined other leaders and captured Gwalior. She died fighting near Gwalior, wearing the dress of a soldier. Even the British officers praised her courage. The poem by Subhadra Kumari Chauhan about her bravery is known to almost every child in north India. Rani Lakshmibai remains a symbol of courage, self respect and love for the motherland."),
("Shaheed Bhagat Singh", "Bhagat Singh is one of the youngest and most loved martyrs of India. He was born in a village in Punjab in a family of freedom fighters. As a boy, he was deeply hurt by the massacre at Jallianwala Bagh, and he decided to give his life for the freedom of India. He joined a group of young revolutionaries who wanted to end British rule. He was a great reader and writer, and he believed that freedom must also mean justice for workers and farmers. To make the deaf hear, he and his friend threw harmless bombs in the Central Assembly and shouted the slogan Inquilab Zindabad. They did not run away and allowed themselves to be arrested. In jail, he went on a long hunger strike for the rights of prisoners. He was hanged along with Rajguru and Sukhdev at a very young age. Their death shook the whole nation and inspired lakhs of young people to join the struggle. Bhagat Singh is remembered as Shaheed e Azam. His courage and his clear ideas still guide the youth of India."),
("Mahatma Gandhi", "Mahatma Gandhi is known as the Father of the Nation. He was born in Porbandar in Gujarat and studied law in England. In South Africa, he faced unfair treatment because of the colour of his skin. There he started his first fight for justice using the path of truth and non violence, which he called satyagraha. After returning to India, he led the farmers of Champaran and Kheda and the mill workers of Ahmedabad. He soon became the leader of the freedom movement. He started the Non Cooperation Movement, the Salt March to Dandi and the Quit India Movement. Millions of common people joined him, and the British found it hard to rule. Gandhiji lived a very simple life. He wore khadi, spun yarn on the charkha and asked people to use goods made in India. He worked against untouchability and for the welfare of villages, women and the poor. He believed that cleanliness is next to godliness. His ideas of truth, non violence and self help have inspired great leaders across the world. His birthday is celebrated as the International Day of Non Violence."),
("Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel", "Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel is known as the Iron Man of India. He was born in Gujarat and became a successful lawyer. He gave up his rich life to join the freedom struggle under Mahatma Gandhi. He led the farmers of Kheda and later the Bardoli satyagraha, where the farmers refused to pay an unfair rise in land tax. The success of Bardoli made him famous, and the women of Bardoli gave him the title of Sardar. After independence, he became the first Deputy Prime Minister and Home Minister of India. At that time, there were more than five hundred princely states, and India faced the risk of breaking into pieces. With great skill and firmness, Sardar Patel brought almost all of these states into the Indian Union. He also laid the base of the all India services, which he called the steel frame of the nation. His birthday is celebrated as National Unity Day. The Statue of Unity in Gujarat, the tallest statue in the world, has been built in his honour. His life teaches us the value of unity and strong will."),
("Swami Vivekananda", "Swami Vivekananda was a great saint and thinker of India. His childhood name was Narendranath, and he was born in Kolkata. He was a bright and curious student who wanted to know the truth about God. He met Sri Ramakrishna, who became his teacher and changed his life. After the death of his teacher, he travelled across India on foot and saw the pain of the poor. He went to America and spoke at the World Parliament of Religions in Chicago. His speech began with the words Sisters and Brothers of America, and the hall rose to clap for a long time. He spoke about the unity of all religions and the wisdom of India. He founded the Ramakrishna Mission to serve the poor, the sick and the needy. He told the youth to have faith in themselves and to work hard. His famous message was, arise, awake and stop not till the goal is reached. His birthday is celebrated as National Youth Day. His ideas still fill young people with energy and the wish to serve the nation."),
("Makar Sankranti", "Makar Sankranti is a harvest festival celebrated in the month of January. On this day, the sun enters the sign of Capricorn and begins its journey towards the north. From this day, the days start to become longer and the cold of winter slowly goes away. The festival is known by different names in different parts of India. It is called Pongal in Tamil Nadu, Lohri is celebrated a day before it in Punjab, Bihu in Assam and Uttarayan in Gujarat. People take a holy dip in rivers like the Ganga and give food and clothes to the poor. Sweets made of sesame and jaggery are eaten and shared, as they keep the body warm in winter. In many places, people eat khichdi on this day. Kite flying is a big part of the festival, and the sky is filled with kites of many colours. Large fairs are held on the banks of rivers. Farmers thank nature for a good harvest. Makar Sankranti teaches us to share, to be thankful and to welcome change with a happy heart."),
("Eid ul Fitr", "Eid ul Fitr is a festival of joy and thanks celebrated by Muslims all over the world. It comes at the end of Ramzan, the holy month in which people keep a fast from dawn till sunset. During Ramzan, people pray more, read the holy book and try to control anger and greed. The festival begins when the new moon is seen. On the morning of Eid, people take a bath, wear new clothes and go to the mosque or the Eidgah for special prayers. After the prayers, they hug each other and say Eid Mubarak. Before the prayers, every family gives a fixed amount of food or money to the poor so that they can also celebrate. In every home, a sweet dish of thin noodles cooked in milk, called sewaiyan, is made. Friends and neighbours of all faiths visit each other and enjoy the food together. Children get gifts and money from elders, which is called Eidi. The festival teaches us patience, kindness and sharing. It also brings people of all communities closer and spreads love and brotherhood in society."),
]
